    "product_id": "00188393-cea5-47df-95b8-b551096dd351",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 100801\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n101 AM PDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* An incoming system will bring widespread precipitation chances and \n  an elevated flash flood risk to the area through the weekend. \n\n* This system will also bring gusty winds to the region over the \n  weekend into early next week.\n\n* Cooler than normal temperatures will stick around through next \n  week. &&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Friday.\n\nWe continue to monitor Rachel in the East Pacific and the incoming \ntrough, currently over British Columbia. As this trough begins to \ndig south today and tomorrow, Rachel will continue tracking \nnortheast towards the coast of Southern California before lifting \ninto southern California and up the Colorado River Valley overnight. \nAs Rachel's circulation approaches the area today, we precipitation \nchances begin to increase in the afternoon and evening. Showers and \nthunderstorms will become more widespread overnight Saturday into \nSunday as the circulation moves overhead. Heavy rain, flash \nflooding, and strong winds will be the primary threats with these \nstorms associated with Rachel's circulation. Additionally, the Storm \nPrediction Center (SPC) has highlighted southern Mohave County, the \nColorado River Valley, and southern Nevada with a 2% tornado risk. \nIf a tornado were to occur, it would be a short-lived spin up on the \nlower end of the EF-scale. The aforementioned trough will push also \npush into the area on Sunday as Rachel moves through. Cold air \naloft, combined with remnant moisture, and dynamic forcing aloft \nwill help fuel widespread storms across the region on Sunday. Given \nthe widespread threat of flash flooding, the Flash Flood Watches for \nmost of the area will remain in effect until early Monday morning. \nPrecipitation will linger through Monday across our eastern zones, \ntapering off on Tuesday. \n\nIn addition to heavy rain and flash flooding, strong southerly winds \nare also expected across the region beginning later today across the \nsouthern Great Basin as the trough begins to dig south. Winds across \nthe southern half of our forecast area will pick up overnight/early \nSunday morning as Rachel's circulation moves through the area. The \nlatest forecast has 40+ mph wind gusts starting earlier than the \nprevious forecast package. Given this earlier onset of strong winds, \nwe have pushed the start time of the Wind Advisory for San \nBernardino, Mohave, and Clark Counties from 5:00 am Sunday to 2:00 \nam Sunday. Overall, wind gusts are expected to peak in the 45 to 55 \nmph range, outside of any convective influence (thunderstorm winds). \nHowever, guidance continues to show a dry slot forming over Mohave \nCounty early Sunday morning as Rachel's circulation moves up the \nColorado River Valley. This will open the door for stronger winds \naloft to mix down to the surface. Winds will be elevated today and \nMonday, peaking on Sunday.\n\nAside from heavy rain, flash flooding, and strong winds, cooler than \nnormal temperatures will also accompany this weekend's unsettled \nweather. Temperatures will drop around 10 to 15 degrees from today \nto tomorrow, and will continue to cool through at least Tuesday. \nBeyond Tuesday, temperatures look to slowly recover during the \nsecond half of the week. However, despite this gradual warm up, \ntemperatures will remain cooler than normal as we head into the \nweekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Expect \nlight southwest winds through the night and into the mid morning on \nSaturday before winds turn more south-southwesterly and gusts above \n20 kts return. These winds increase into the mid afternoon with \ngusts peaking around 30 kts as light showers begin to approach the \nvalley. Shower activity in the area increases slightly into the \nevening with gusts decreasing slightly. A line of showers arrives \ninto the late night on Saturday as winds reduce and directions \nbecome more variable.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Mostly clear skies \ncontinue into Saturday afternoon. Mostly light winds are expected \nthrough the night Gusty south to southwest winds arrive into \nSaturday afternoon with some chances for passing showers. Gusty \nwinds look to last into the night for all but DAG which sees some \nsheltering from the stronger south winds. Expect a light of showers \nto arrive in the evening for KIFP and KEED with these showers \narriving to Las Vegas Valley TAF sites into the night.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Stessman\nAVIATION...Murdock\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
